# /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-stick-remount
#
# Lets the server briefly make a mounted stick writable, so it can save
# what it learned - the library manifest it builds while scanning (see
# stick-manifest.js) and the owner's favourites (favourites.js).
#
# usb-mount.sh mounts sticks read-only on purpose: a DJ yanks a stick
# without warning, and exFAT corrupts badly when interrupted mid-write.
# That protection is kept - the stick is read-only at every instant
# except the few milliseconds a save actually takes, and the server
# remounts it back immediately afterwards.
#
# The alternative shapes were considered and rejected (2026-08-29):
# mounting read-write permanently gives a yank the whole session to
# land badly, and a bind-mount arrangement exposing only .waxcode still
# leaves the filesystem writable underneath while being far more
# machinery to set up and tear down per stick.
#
# Scoped to remount only, and only under /media/pidvs/. Not a general
# mount capability - that would let any bug here mount arbitrary
# devices anywhere.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp pidvs-sudoers-stick-remount /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-stick-remount
#   sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-stick-remount
#   sudo visudo -c

__BOX_USER__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mount -o remount\,rw /media/pidvs/*, /usr/bin/mount -o remount\,ro /media/pidvs/*
